Expected elements: present

At 1920 this is a genuinely dense tool — 36 code lines, 7 frames, 10 values and 14 metadata rows on one screen — and the contrast survey is clean: across 26 sampled text elements the minimum is 4.68:1 ("opcodes", x1466-1520 y107-118). Density is not the problem; allocation is.

My numbers hold. Column split 913/608/383px (was 912/607/382). Call Trace tabbed region y68-669 = 602px, content ends y≈330 → 339px empty = 56.3% (was 57.0%). Values pane y674-1075 = 402px, last row ends y≈950 → 125px = 31.1% (was 29.8%). Middle-column slack 464px = 46.0% of its 1008px (was 467px/45.9%). The fade did not move the numbers; it changed what they look like. You can now see which panes are truncated, so the imbalance is legible rather than inferable — that strengthens the re-weighting case, it does not weaken it.

The fade reads as "there is more", not as a cut. But it is not the same ramp everywhere: Transaction ≈14px, last line holding 15.5:1 → 4.92:1; Code ≈24px, starting above line 61's cap-height and driving its gutter number 4.36:1 → 2.24:1. The 2.19:1 figure is real, but it measures the gutter, not the code (4.61:1). Anchor the ramp to the last full line's baseline, as Transaction already does.

Top fixes: (1) move ~120px from the middle column into the Transaction pane; (2) unify the ramp across panes.

Rating: 7/10

